# Branches and worktrees

## Never push to main

- Do **not** commit directly on `main` for feature work
- Do **not** push to `main` — ever
- All changes land via **PR from a new branch**

Workflow: new branch → work in worktree → commit → push branch → open PR.

## Prefer worktrees

Use git worktrees for almost all non-trivial work. Keeps `main` checkout clean and lets multiple tasks run in parallel.

**Location:** `.worktrees/` (already in `.gitignore`).

```bash
# Create worktree + branch from latest main
git fetch origin main
git worktree add .worktrees/my-feature -b feat/my-feature origin/main
cd .worktrees/my-feature
cargo build   # or project-appropriate setup
```

Before creating a worktree, verify `.worktrees/` is ignored:

```bash
git check-ignore -q .worktrees
```

If not ignored, add to `.gitignore` and commit that fix first.

## Branch naming

Prefix + kebab-case slug:

| Prefix | Use |
|---|---|
| `feature/` | New capability |
| `fix/` | Bug fix |
| `refactor/` | Structural cleanup, no user-visible change |
| `spec/` | Spec / design doc work |
| `build/` | CI, deps, Docker |

Worktree folder name should match the branch when practical (e.g. `.worktrees/fix-pings-data-dir` on `fix/pings-data-dir`).

## When to skip a worktree

Tiny one-line fixes in an already-open worktree branch are fine. For anything new — use a worktree.

## Push and PR

```bash
git push -u origin HEAD
gh pr create --title "..." --body "$(cat <<'EOF'
## Summary
...

## Test plan
...
EOF
)"
```

Only push the **feature branch**, never `main`.
